WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.920
Hi, I’m Thomas

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.000
One of the things I love most about film as
a medium is that a filmmaker can use the visual

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.140
language of film let you see the progression
of the story.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:19.210
Like this moment in The Godfather, where Michael
has to make a phone call about his father.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.779
This phone call ends up being the incident
that will change things for Michael forever,

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:28.939
and his physical separation from his girlfriend
Kay as he makes the call foreshadows the relational

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:31.079
separation that begins at this moment.

00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:36.040
It never has to be called attention to, or
stated explicitly, and the audience might

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.460
not even notice it, but subconsciously it
reinforces the story visually.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:46.530
But the Godfather isn’t the Al Pacino film
I’m going to talk about in this video, let’s

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.960
take a look at The Panic In Needle Park and
how it uses a tryptych to mark the major progressions

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.079
in the story.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.440
The Panic in Needle Park follows the story
of Helen, a homeless girl whos friendship

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.010
and romance with Bobby leads her into heroin
addiction.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.120
Spoilers ahead.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.610
Director Jerry Schatzberg uses three key shots
to bookmark each major moment in the arc of

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.770
the story.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:28.740
In the first we see Helen alone on the train.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.290
This shot establishes for us her problem,
she’s on her own, and unlike the people

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:45.530
crowded around her moments before, she has
nowhere to go, nowhere to be.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.409
Then Helen meets Bobby, and their relationship
solves some of her problems but she eventually

00:01:50.409 --> 00:02:09.080
gives in to the temptation of the constant
exposure and availability of heroin.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.760
Then we get the second key shot towards the
middle of the film.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.680
We can see the closeness of their relationship,
and the seriousness of her decision to step

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.930
fully into Bobby’s world.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:25.510
It shows us how their romantic relationship
inseparably linked to Helen’s drug use.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.640
We see her shame, and Bobby’s dread as he
envisions the future.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.540
And how in a tragic way, now that she’s
using, they can finally share a big part of

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.700
Bobby’s life, and they’re closer than
they’ve ever been.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.250
But the story doesn’t end there.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:54.120
As the film progresses their drug use starts
to supersede their relationship.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.600
And in a final attempt to escape from heroin
and by extension Bobby, Helen ends up betraying

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.190
him and ratting him out to the police.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:34.470
The final shot alludes to the finality of
this progression.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.069
As Helen chases Bobby, it’s clear that any
remnant of love has long been overshadowed

00:03:39.069 --> 00:04:00.030
by the damage heroin has done to their relationship.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.970
Helen seeks forgiveness for her betrayal but
as her relationship with Bobby became inseparable

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:09.920
from her relationship to heroin, we realize
her reunion with Bobby probably means her

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:29.010
return to using.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:34.290
When you look at all the shots together as
a triptych you can see the whole arc of the

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.580
story.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:41.920
Helen is drawn to Bobby out of love, that
love leads her to drug use, and finally they’re

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.440
together but no semblance of love remains,
their relationship is both destroyed and held

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.760
together by their mutual need for a fix.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.040
When I think of The Panic in Needle Park,
I think of these three shots.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.970
They dug into my brain and didn’t let go.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.160
What makes these three shots stick out?

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.261
The first key shot is a long shot that holds
after a series of a few shorter shots, this

00:05:06.261 --> 00:05:11.770
combined with its focus on a single subject
amidst turmoil and motion causes the moment

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:14.270
to stick in the minds of the viewer.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:19.510
The second shot is also a longer shot, but
it most obviously signals to the audience

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:24.890
the significance but cutting from a very wide
angle to an extreme close up.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:29.740
This shot is the closest the camera has ever
been to their faces together, it’s the most

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.350
intimate moment we experience between them.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:43.630
To further emphasize this particular moment
the noise of the street falls away.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:48.630
The final shot starts with an empty frame,
and then reveals the two characters we’ve

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:55.030
been focused on and care about, slowly answering
the question we all have in our minds.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:00.050
What will happen to Bobby and Helen, will
their relationship work out?

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:05.240
Notice how what clues we get towards the answer
come from the blocking of the scene.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:10.340
It’s a long shot, and when it cuts to black,
the image of the two of them together sticks

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.090
in your mind.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.420
You can form triptychs like this from other
films as well.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:22.850
Returning to The Godfather, we can use three
key shots to show the main arc of the story.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.810
Often the memorable shots from a film are
memorable simply because they looked cool.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.340
But what if you can use single shots to give
your audience signposts along the way.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:38.771
A movie is experienced linearly as you watch
it and all at once as you remember it, and

00:06:38.771 --> 00:06:43.930
I think good directors know how to craft a
film that will be powerful both as you experience

00:06:43.930 --> 00:07:19.970
it, and as you think back over it in the hours
and days after watching it.

